; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; AUTHORED -- there is no upstream .bddl for this task, and the `_bulkhead`
; Python suite this began life as predates the bolted-port variant and never
; shipped in this repository. What it inherits, it inherits from that variant:
; every threshold, verbatim. It ships beside this document as `port`.
;
; WHAT MAKES THIS ONE INTERESTING. `port` mates a plug into a receptacle that
; is BOLTED DOWN. This one mates it into a receptacle that is being HELD --
; a free 90 g panel-mount bulkhead, taken off a parts-tray cradle
; by the support arm and carried into the air. Both hands lift clear, the mate
; happens with neither part touching anything else, and then both descend still
; mated and let go.
;
; That is the arrangement the reference bulkhead sim ABANDONED: every lifted
; carry it measured popped the grip. What makes it hold here is that the grip is
; a flat pinch across the 42 mm barrel rather than a pad grip on the nose, and
; that the receptacle's cradle has no side cheeks for the jaws to collide with.
;
; The mate line is the block port's own. `stage.load_bulkhead` returns the mesh
; already placed so its slot axis lies on that line with the mouth faces
; coplanar, which is why every threshold below is the one `port`
; stated: same plug, same fit, same numbers. Only the thing on the other end has
; changed, and whether it is holding still.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(define (problem INSERT_THE_PLUG_INTO_THE_HELD_BULKHEAD)


  (:language insert the USB-C plug into the bulkhead the other hand is holding)

  (:staging
    (:scene bench_075)
    ; The two arms are solid to each other. Each arm stays internally
    ; filtered; see EmbodimentSpec.cross_arm_collisions.
    ;
    ; **This variant does not survive it as shipped, and the number is
    ; 3/3 -> 0/3.** Measured three ways on one box, everything else
    ; identical: HEAD 3/3, arms solid 0/3, arms solid switched back off with
    ; every other change of this branch still in place 3/3. So it is this
    ; field and nothing else.
    ;
    ; The mechanism is the graph's own sequence. `brace` puts the support
    ; arm under the bulkhead and *holds it in the air*, then `insert` sends
    ; the work arm to the plug -- through the space the support arm is now
    ; standing in. With the arms filtered the work arm passed through it;
    ; solid, it stalls short and `insert_sg.check_grasp` reports
    ; `route="missed", held=false, jaw_gap 8.0 mm`: the jaws closed on
    ; nothing. It is not a grasp fault and not a collision the plan knows
    ; about, because nothing ever measured the two arms against each other.
    ;
    ; The repair is `cloth_fold`'s, which met the same wall: plan the hold
    ; against a clearance target and pick the elbow branch that leaves the
    ; corridor free (see FoldRoutine's HOLD_LINE_STEPS and
    ; ARM_CLEARANCE_TARGET). Left on rather than quietly reverted, because
    ; a pairing that cannot state where its two arms are is the thing being
    ; fixed, and a green number bought by not looking is what it was worth.
    ;
    ; **Re-measured 2026-08-31 and the 0/3 did not reproduce: `gt` is 4/4
    ; here with this field on.** So what it costs is not a fixed penalty, it
    ; is a sensitivity -- and the way to see it is to add a station.
    ; `graphs/mate` puts the work hand 120 mm above the bore line to take a
    ; camera look, a pose `gt` never visits and nothing checks the support
    ; elbow against, and this variant scores **2/6 there with the same
    ; privileged ground truth `gt` scores 4/4 with**. The corridor is still
    ; there; it is just wide enough for one choreography and not the other.
    (:embodiment yam_i2rt (:work-arm right) (:support-arm left) (:arms-solid true))
    ; As `port`, and for the same reason: a 0.15 mm clearance fit
    ; is commanded in task space or not at all.
    (:control-mode ee_pose_abs)
    ; The reference's stiffened servos: the task carries them, not the
    ; embodiment, because a 1600-gain arm is an insertion fact -- at the
    ; MJCF's own 300 the TCP lags ~10 mm and the fit never lines up.
    (:arm-actuation (:ke 1600) (:kd 70))
    (:gripper-actuation (:ke 20000) (:kd 20))
    ; NOT `usbc_960hz@v1`. Same rates, same contact settings, twice the
    ; constraint ceilings -- three contacting bodies instead of two, and a
    ; barrel whose contact count peaks on curved faces. See
    ; tasks/usbc_insert/staging.py, which owns both.
    (:physics usbc_bulkhead_960hz@v1)
    ; The perception rig, not the reference multiview. The reference's three
    ; views were aimed by somebody who already knew where the parts were --
    ; `mating` frames 200 mm of the mate line and nothing else -- which is the
    ; knowledge a perception body is not allowed to have. This adds a top-down
    ; over the bench and an eye-in-hand on the work arm, and the second is the
    ; one this variant cannot do without: the receptacle spends the middle of
    ; the episode 120 mm off the table, where every placed eye in this cell is
    ; looking at a bench that no longer has it on.
    (:cameras usbc_perception@v1)
    ; THIRTY DRAWS, NOT THIRTY REPEATS. `fixed` was right while this variant was
    ; a reference reproduction and every number in the graph READMEs was a
    ; measurement of one layout run again. It is wrong for a policy that claims
    ; to find its parts: a fixed spawn cannot tell perception from memory.
    ;
    ; `staging:USBC_BENCH` puts the plug where a hand would have left it --
    ; 18 mm of position and 14 degrees of heading -- and nudges this free
    ; receptacle by the slop in its own cradle, 4 mm and 3 degrees. See
    ; tasks/usbc_insert/staging.py for all four numbers and why they are the
    ; sizes they are.
    (:episodes (:count 30) (:seed 0) (:sampler staging:USBC_BENCH))
    ; Longer than `port`'s 6000: this episode has to take the
    ; receptacle off its cradle, lift both parts clear, mate, descend and
    ; retract before the verdict can be read with nobody holding anything.
    (:max-steps 9000)
    ; Two arms: the graph lays out an action vector per arm and parks the one
    ; it is not inserting with. It does not manipulate with it, but it does
    ; need it to exist -- so a one-armed robot is refused here rather than
    ; discovered mid-rollout.
    (:arms 2)
  )

  (:objects
    ; BOTH ARE FREE BODIES, and that is the whole task. In `port`
    ; the receptacle was a fixture; here it is a thing with mass that has to be
    ; picked up, held steady against the insertion push, and put back down.
    ; Receptacle first: declaration order is body-handle order, and the cables
    ; below root a chain on each of these by label.
    bulkhead - usb_c_bulkhead
    plug     - usb_c_plug
    ; THE SUITE'S OWN ENTITIES, which no generic kind expresses and no clause
    ; names: the probe rig (fingertip blades and the jaw calibration -- surgery
    ; on the hand rather than a thing on the bench), a cable rooted on each
    ; connector (the honest load an insertion carries, and why this document
    ; stages doubled constraint ceilings), and the receptacle's detent (the
    ; retention spring this contact stack cannot express, modeled as a
    ; criterion-gated weld -- see assets/entity.py). Built after the bodies
    ; they attach to, in this order.
    rig      - usbc_rig
    cables   - usbc_cables
    detent   - usbc_detent
  )

  (:fixtures
    ; The bench the plug starts on, and the parts-tray cradle the bulkhead rests
    ; in -- two plain boxes computed off the asserted barrel and flange radii,
    ; with deliberately no side cheeks and no axial stop (see assets/scenery.py).
    ; Nothing here scores against any of them.
    bench        - usbc_bench
    cradle_front - usbc_cradle_front
    cradle_rear  - usbc_cradle_rear
  )

  ; No regions: every quantity is measured against the bulkhead's own declared
  ; bore, which travels with it. That is the point of stating a feature in the
  ; body's frame rather than as a box in the world -- this receptacle spends the
  ; middle of the episode 120 mm off the table, and a region would have had to
  ; follow it there.

  ; -------------------------------------------------------------------------
  ; No init constraint. Both parts spawn where the reference assembly puts them:
  ; the plug on the bench, the bulkhead resting in its cradle with its slot axis
  ; already on the mate line. Nothing is latched, gripped or pre-posed.
  (:init)

  ; -------------------------------------------------------------------------
  ; FIVE CLAUSES: `port`'s four, plus the one this variant
  ; exists to add.
  ;
  ; (insertion plug bulkhead.bore) >= 0.0104
  ;     Tab-tip depth past the mouth: 10.4 mm of the connector's 13 mm bore.
  ;     The connector's number, not this scene's -- 0.8 x bore_depth, and
  ;     parts.py's `gates()` is what keeps the literal here from drifting from
  ;     it.
  ;
  ; (insertion:lat plug bulkhead.bore) <= 0.0087931792
  ;     Depth only counts inside the mouth's half-diagonal. The geometric guard
  ;     against scoring a plug that slid PAST the receptacle face -- which reads
  ;     as deep as a seated one along the axis, and is the failure a
  ;     depth-only goal cannot see. The block port's half-diagonal, because the
  ;     mouths are coplanar and the plug is the same plug.
  ;
  ; (speed plug) <= 0.05
  ;     Mated, not mid-flight. Sharper here than it is in `port`: the pair is
  ;     in the air for most of this episode, so a depth reading taken while both
  ;     arms are still moving is a reading of a mate that has not settled.
  ;
  ; (not (gripped plug.body)) and (tcp-distance plug) > 0.05
  ;     LET GO, AND BACK AWAY. Two clauses because opening the jaws is not the
  ;     same as letting go of the claim, and with the receptacle held in the air
  ;     by the other arm the difference decides the task: a plug still between
  ;     the fingers at depth is being held in position by two servos rather than
  ;     retained by the socket, which is precisely what is under test.
  ;
  ;     The retract is the second half of it. A hand parked around a seated plug
  ;     with its fingers open is one twitch from holding it, and -- the reason
  ;     the threshold exists at all -- an episode that terminated the instant the
  ;     jaws opened would end BEFORE the retract, so the recording would never
  ;     show the plug standing on its own. 50 mm makes the verdict and the video
  ;     agree.
  ;
  ;     Read against the TCP, which is where `tcp-distance` measures from: on
  ;     this gripper the grasp site is 134.7 mm along the tool body's local -z,
  ;     so a distance to the body ORIGIN reads ~137 mm with the pads still
  ;     touching the part. A 50 mm gate on that is satisfied on frame one and
  ;     gates nothing.
  ;
  ; NOTHING LATCHES. A mate that pops apart on the retract is scored where it
  ; ends up -- and popping apart on the retract is the exact failure the
  ; reference sim hit and gave up on, so a `once` here would hide the thing this
  ; variant was built to measure.
  ; (insertion:tilt plug bulkhead.bore) <= 0.0873
  ;     SEATED, NOT HANGING. Five degrees, in radians. Depth is an axial
  ;     PROJECTION, so a plug that pivots inside the bore instead of staying on
  ;     its axis reports as a plug that stayed in -- and this receptacle's slot
  ;     is 8.00 mm tall where a real USB-C jack is 5.20, which leaves 1.6 mm a
  ;     side of rotation budget. Recorded through a release on the shipped `gt`
  ;     graph: 0.99 degrees while the jaws hold it, 10.1 within eighteen frames
  ;     of them opening, settling at 10.9 -- scored reward 1.00 the whole way,
  ;     because depth and lat both still passed. Free of the arms entirely a
  ;     seated plug reaches 17.6, the slot's own geometric stop.
  ;
  ;     Five degrees admits a genuinely seated mate (measured 0.99-1.44 while
  ;     held) and refuses one hanging off its mouth. This clause is the whole
  ;     difference between "the plug is in the socket" and "the plug is
  ;     somewhere past the mouth plane", which is all the other four can say.
  (:goal
    (And
      (>= (insertion plug bulkhead.bore) 0.0104)
      (<= (insertion:lat plug bulkhead.bore) 0.0087931792)
      (<= (insertion:tilt plug bulkhead.bore) 0.0873)
      (<= (speed plug) 0.05)
      (not (gripped plug.body))
      (> (tcp-distance plug) 0.05)
    )
  )

  ; -------------------------------------------------------------------------
  ; Depth over the gate, masked by the lateral guard -- `port`'s
  ; reward exactly, and deliberately NOT extended to cover the release.
  ;
  ; The two are different questions. Reward measures how far in the plug got;
  ; release and retract are conditions on WHEN that reading counts, and folding
  ; them into the quantity would pay a policy for backing away from a plug it
  ; never seated.
  ; The mask carries the tilt guard as well as the lateral one, for the reason
  ; the goal states it: without it the term pays a policy for a plug lying
  ; across the mouth at ten degrees exactly as much as for a seated one, and
  ; the scalar goes on reporting 1.00 while the mate visibly hangs.
  (:reward
    (progress (insertion plug bulkhead.bore)
              (:gate 0.0104)
              (:gated-by (And (<= (insertion:lat plug bulkhead.bore) 0.0087931792)
                              (<= (insertion:tilt plug bulkhead.bore) 0.0873)))))

  ; -------------------------------------------------------------------------
  ; Recorded every step, gating nothing. `mate_gap` is the one to watch on this
  ; variant: it is the measure that shows a carry losing the fit gradually
  ; rather than at once.
  (:observe
    (gripped plug.body)
    (insertion:mate_gap plug bulkhead.bore)
    (speed bulkhead)
  )

  ; -------------------------------------------------------------------------
  ; A graph, and no routine. The reference's bimanual *script* is not ported --
  ; it was written against a policy base this stack has since rewritten, and a
  ; scripted solution transcribed against the wrong base is a solution that runs
  ; and is not the one that was measured. What ships instead is the graph, which
  ; is a better fit for this variant anyway: the correction it makes after
  ; grasping is exactly what an open-loop stroke cannot do.
  (:artifacts
    (:tools "tools:register_usbc_tools")
    (:graph gt "graphs/gt")
    ; `mate` is `gt`'s choreography behind a perception slot, and it serves the
    ; bolted `port` variant from the same workflow. It is NOT a replacement:
    ; `gt` is the measured baseline and its README carries the numbers.
    (:graph mate "graphs/mate")
  )

  (:provenance
    (:tier original)
    (:source "Authored for this corpus, from a pre-port _bulkhead Python suite that never
              shipped in this repository; thresholds are the usbc_insert/port document's,
              verbatim, and the mate line is the reference block port's.")
    (:note "Bulkhead.stl at scale 2, pre-placed so its slot axis spawns on the block port's
            bore line, carrying the reference sim's 90 g ground-truth mass, resting in a
            two-step cradle that is a rest and nothing more. The cradle has deliberately no
            side cheeks and no axial stop: cheeks stand exactly where the support jaws close
            across the 42 mm barrel, and reacting the insertion push is the support arm's job,
            which is what makes this bimanual rather than scenery. The part measures 0.05
            degrees of tilt over a settle without them. Thresholds are the block variant's
            throughout -- same plug, coplanar mouths, one mate line.")
  )
)
